00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.530
The mapping of the Metropolitan center

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.460
and its outlying periphery
is both geographically real

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.963
and historic construed.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.280
The associations implied by these terms

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.440
have been highly
influential in the writing

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.453
of both art and music
histories of the Renaissance.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.040
At its most influential level,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.310
the terms have been deployed
in historical accounts

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:39.690
to control the borders of the field,

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:43.210
to make decisions about what
to admit and what to own it,

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.150
and to provide a accounting of history.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:49.170
One that is fundamentally
narrated from the center,

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.040
often through qualitative comparisons

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.490
with the so-called periphery.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.850
In a famous statement by
the British art historian,

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.210
Kenneth Clark,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.550
the center was the place
for the creation of art

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.440
leading to that style
becoming the dominant one.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.850
It is metropolitan at its center,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.070
but as it began to spread
out to the periphery,

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.563
it becomes more provincial.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.960
In short, a style does
not develop universally

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.560
over a region.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.960
It is the metropole,

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.260
a single unit that
stimulates its progression.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.010
This view was a point of departure

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.660
for two highly influential articles

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:26.500
by the Italian historians Carlo Ginzburg

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:28.960
and Enrico Castelnuovo

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.470
who did not see a predictable
transmission of style

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.140
from the center to the periphery,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.290
but rather the emergence
of a power dynamic

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.380
between the two areas in which the

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.440
so-called rich-heartedness,
this is their word,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.540
of the periphery is a
rejection of innovation

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.990
and resistance to the center.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.420
In the field of Renaissance musicology,

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:51.930
the notion of center and periphery

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.610
has essentially guided the manner

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:57.370
in which we map music history
from the early 15th century

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:58.723
to around 1600.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.410
It is a story of courtly centers
of patronage like Ferrara

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.400
or ecclesiastical
institutions like the Vatican,

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.350
rarely venturing further
out into domestic music.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:13.020
The provinces or cities in
the Venito not named Venice

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.423
or ones in Lombardy, not named Milan.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:20.640
This model has effectively
subordinated entire repertories,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.243
even urban ones like
urban instrumental music.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.960
It has been used to ensure
the primacy of notated

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.740
so-called permanent works

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.420
over improvisational
so-called evanescent pieces,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.240
even though improvisation was
one of the central techniques

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:38.823
used to teach composition in
the 15th and 16th centuries.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.040
And the center periphery
model has also been used

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.130
to prioritize certain regions of activity.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.923
Most notably courtly and
urban ecclesiastical cultures.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.560
In this talk, I will suggest a revision

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.420
to the notion of center and periphery

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.950
by offering a new map of
Renaissance music history,

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.760
based on the concept of polycentricity

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.090
in which many centers can
operate simultaneously

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.070
characterized by both conformity

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.440
as well as individual and local styles,

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.893
both written and unwritten,
vocal or instrumental.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:15.960
I will focus on three case studies.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:19.210
The first is on the 16th
century Dutch composer,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.760
Clemens non Papa in order to show

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.140
how the dominant center periphery model

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.470
in Renaissance musicology has affected

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:27.770
the reception of his work.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.790
I will then move to a case
study about instrumental music,

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:33.380
which provides an excellent model

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.003
for a polycentric approach.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.390
And my third example,

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.340
I will suggest how a polycentric approach

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:42.450
can even be influential in editorial work

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.250
through the example of
a new complete edition

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.600
of the works of the 16th century lutenist

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.963
Francesco da Milano,
that I've recently begun.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:55.240
The Renaissance composer Jacobus Clemens

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:59.060
known by his contemporaries
as Clemens non Papa.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:02.060
In other words, Clemens not the Pope

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.850
as if anyone would've
mistaken him on the street

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:08.090
as the reigning Pope
Clement the seventh, Medici.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:11.140
Was born in the Netherlands around 1515,

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:13.250
and became one of the best known

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:16.620
and most influential
composers of the 16th century.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.870
But his music is barely known today,

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.570
even to specialists of the period.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.420
Clemens has barely given a name check

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:27.050
in the standard histories
of reference works.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.150
It is rare as well to hear his
music performed or recorded

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:34.250
outside of a few pieces, even
by musicians who specialize

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.611
in this period and try to
explore many uncharted areas

00:09:37.611 --> 00:09:39.653
of the 16th century repertoire.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:44.640
Clement's contemporaries did
not see it this way however,

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.810
and this is an important
consideration that should push us

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:49.840
to revise the manner in which we map

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.463
our Renaissance music histories.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.570
He composed an astonishing amount

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.170
in every major genre of Renaissance music,

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.260
mass, motet, French chanson.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.750
And in compliance with the reformation

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.483
he composed over 100
Psalm settings in Dutch.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:08.160
His music sees us vividly
on the texts he chose

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.103
in order to create sounds
scapes of drama and depth.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:15.470
In addition, his work
was arranged repeatedly

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:19.560
by instrumentalists, lutenists
and wind bands mostly

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.970
who not only extended his fame all the way

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:25.010
to the end of the 16th century
by their many versions,

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.810
mainly for lute of his vocal music,

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:30.210
but also dispersed his music for a rainbow

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:32.120
of different classes of musicians,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.210
civic wind bands, lutenists, *The Waylors.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.670
Lutenists in Spain played
a similar instrument,

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.460
keyboard players, professionals,

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:42.010
and amateurs throughout Europe.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.440
So here is a composer who
achieved considerable fame

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.320
in his day, but of whose
vast musical output

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.310
numbering over 500 works,

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:52.930
there's only faint familiarity

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:55.360
by Renaissance
musicologists and performers

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.283
outside of a few dozen pieces.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:01.240
Then there is the problem of transmission.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:04.060
His music survives in many formats,

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.010
printed and manuscript
sources in Dutch, Latin,

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.620
and French in many
different types of notation.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.520
And we must consider not only the pieces

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.010
that he wrote himself, but also
the hundreds of arrangements

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.780
made of his music by other musicians,

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.220
which introduced one of
the central blind spots

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:25.383
of Renaissance musicology
that I will return to later.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.810
In short, his considerable output

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.980
has yet to receive systematic
and consistent attention

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.020
and overlooking that would be
inconceivable in art history

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.930
for a painter that was
so prolific, popular

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.773
and whose work remained in
the rotation for decades.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.640
Nevertheless, one wonders how Clemens

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.570
whose works were standards
on the Hip Parade

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:50.670
of the 16th century, is a
mere dot, a gas station icon

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.593
plotted on the accepted
map of Renaissance music.

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:57.930
There are several reasons for this.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.930
The first is a conventional
but increasingly problematic

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:04.483
map-oriented history of Renaissance music.

00:12:07.644 --> 00:12:09.960
In this account, the
central musical language

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:12.570
of the Renaissance
develops from about 1420

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:17.570
to around 1477 with composers
born and musically trained

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.390
in what was then northern Burgundy

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:21.740
and what is now the area

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:24.140
comprising Northern France
and Southern Belgium.

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:29.570
This musical language, its genres

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:33.600
and its compositional practices
were then transmitted south

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.500
over the Alps through the
circulation of manuscripts,

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.280
but more importantly by the recruitment

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.310
of many of these Burgundian composers

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:46.310
by Italian patrons to
cities like Milan, Ferrara,

00:12:46.341 --> 00:12:50.843
Florence, Rome and to some degree Venice.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:56.500
In these mostly Ducal cities
led by a ruling family

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.780
considerable economic
resources were devoted

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.670
to this recruitment of
northern composers and singers.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.780
The assembling of choirs,
the training of musicians,

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.410
the production of manuscripts,
and for the benefits

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.850
normally provided to those
elite pro-Gandhian musicians

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:15.560
now in their employ, a
salary, lodging, horse,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:19.823
wine, wheat and grain and
very often a generous pension.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.460
Few of these composers
remain in Italy for good

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.300
and benefiting from the fierce competition

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:29.870
among patrons for their musical services,

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:33.303
most of these made periodic
returns to the north.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.410
They accepted positions along the way

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:38.570
and added more landmarks
to the historical map

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:40.630
as they personally transmitted their music

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:43.570
along their travel groups
and composed new works

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:46.450
that were often aligned with
local musical traditions

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.023
or honored particular patrons.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.510
The travels of Josquin des Prez,

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:55.090
easily the most famous
composer of this period

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.761
and the first one to
achieve international fame,

00:13:57.761 --> 00:13:59.343
offers a case in point.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:04.513
Josquin is born in 1450 up
here in Northern France.

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:09.770
By 1477, we find him in Aix-en-Provence

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:11.853
working for the Duke René of Anjou.

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:21.047
In 1484, we find him working
for the Sforza Dukes in Milan.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:27.180
There's a reference to him
working in Hungary in 1485.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.003
A reference that is quite
persuasive actually.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:35.033
In 1489, he accepts a
position with the papal court.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:40.403
He returns to France working
for Louis the 12th in Paris,

00:14:42.634 --> 00:14:47.634
In 1503 we find him in the
estate court in Ferrara

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:54.480
and from 1504 to 1521, he
returns close to his birthplace

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.550
in *Cool Bay, Northern France,

00:14:57.550 --> 00:14:59.250
where he remained until his death.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.580
But even then he was
directing the circulation

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:04.360
of his manuscripts throughout Europe,

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.470
and almost had a kind of individual

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:09.860
manuscript production organization.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.570
This gives you a good idea
of the types of travels

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.460
of a famous composer in Italy many times,

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.763
but then finding other positions as well.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.960
With Italian courts now acknowledged

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.740
as the main musical centers
in the 16th century,

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:28.600
the north retreats into
musicological periphery,

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.983
carrying Clemens with it.

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:33.240
Clemens was born somewhere
in Flanders and even though

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.340
hardly any biographical
information survives,

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:39.720
he does not seem to have
ventured far from home

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.800
unlike many of his
remarkable contemporaries.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:46.060
Letters suggest that
he became unemployable

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:49.350
because he was often or even mostly drunk.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.210
He seems to have lurched from job to job.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.700
Maybe this is why he's
known as Clemens non Papa

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.600
perhaps after he was drinking,
he put on papal clothing

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.283
and walked down the street
from tavern to tavern.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:06.180
From beginning with a position in Bruges,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.800
he bounced around several smaller cities,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.530
but he was successful in the
world of publishing music

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.190
and writing works that had
immediate and lasting appeal

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.923
in French, Latin and Dutch.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.310
In addition to the way
that the musicological

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:24.220
historical mapping cramped
Clemens' place in music history,

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.590
a second reason is a result
of another kind of mapping.

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:30.260
One that involves time, chronology

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.723
and the notion of progress.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.870
An unfortunate but durable
musicological narrative

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.410
of 16th century musical progress,

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:41.230
places Clemens historically
within a distinct group

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:45.150
of so-called post Josquin,
quote unquote composers.

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.850
Sentencing him to a transitional
period of music history

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.770
that was characterized not
by innovation and craft,

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:54.490
but by playing to the demands
of the commercial market

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:57.010
during this first boom age of printing

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:00.090
and by an extended formalistic application

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:02.000
of the Renaissance contrapuntal technique

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:04.293
of what's called pervasive imitation.

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:11.213
Following, but never succeeding Josquin,

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:16.170
this group of so-called
post-Josquin composers

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.110
generally appear as a lost generation.

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:22.060
So Clemens is born between 1510 and 1515,

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.200
Josquin dies in 1521,

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.660
But following Josquin's death,

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.310
he becomes more famous than ever.

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:30.380
We have this Josquin afterglow

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:32.390
that continues well into the 1560s

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:35.340
in which, Josquin's catalog of works

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.760
increases exponentially
mostly by misattributions

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.930
or people wanting to place their music

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:42.930
under the name of Josquin.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.830
But because of Josquin's
enormous popularity

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:47.170
during this period,

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:49.600
the composers who were
contemporary in this period,

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.030
were placed by musicologists

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:53.120
in what's called the post-Josquin period.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:58.013
So they exist in the time of
the same Josquin afterglow.

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:01.840
Clemens dies in 1555,
but that is by no means

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.833
when Clemens' music also came to an end.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.300
In the 1560s, the next great,

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:10.670
as far as musicology is concerned,

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:13.750
composer after Josquin was Palestrina

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:17.000
and Palestrina dominates
this age of the 1560s.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.810
And again, it further
cramps Clemens' career

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.983
within this post-Josquin generation.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:29.463
But the arrangements of
Clemens' music reach a peak,

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:33.270
starting almost 10 years after his death

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.830
and lasting for another 20 years.

00:18:35.830 --> 00:18:39.510
There's an enormous amount
of arrangements of Clemens

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:43.050
as instrumentalists contemporize his music

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:46.593
for the last few decades
of the 16th century.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:52.117
So Clemens was essentially
part of a lost generation

00:18:52.117 --> 00:18:54.150
who essentially kept the seat warm

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:56.200
for the great counter
reformation composers,

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:57.963
Palestrina and *Lawzut.

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:00.730
The aesthetic quality of Clemens' output

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:03.260
became and remains grossly undervalued

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:06.540
in this pervasive and
discontinuous historical narrative.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:09.030
One that only took into
account his sacred music,

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.770
not the larger impact his music had

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:14.040
through printing and as a
source for the arrangements

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.183
by other musicians.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:19.190
As an example, one does not
need to look any further

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:22.077
than the discussion of
Clemens in Gustave Reese's

00:19:22.077 --> 00:19:24.890
"Music in the Renaissance" of 1954

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:27.530
and subsequently revised in 1959.

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:30.170
The foundational text about
Renaissance music history

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:33.820
in English equal in importance
to Frederick Hart's book

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.323
on Italian Renaissance art.

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:41.800
Reese discusses Clemens
in a post Josquin chapter

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:46.590
tellingly subtitled "Richafort
and some lesser men."

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:48.090
a group that includes Clemens.

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.070
Reese drew the map of Renaissance music

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.900
into two large geographical areas.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:56.070
The first focused on the
central musical language

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:58.150
of the 15th and 16th centuries,

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:02.000
geographically positioned
exclusively in Burgundy and Italy.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:05.810
The second which he called
music of other lands,

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:07.840
lumped together everything
and everybody else

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.803
in England, Spain, Germany
and Eastern Europe.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:14.270
Reese's division of history reminds us

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:18.320
of Saul Steinberg's famous
1976 New Yorker cartoon

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.310
of the way new yorkers view their city

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:21.810
against the rest of the world.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:24.410
The notions of central and peripheral

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.420
are clearly embedded here,

00:20:26.420 --> 00:20:27.950
both with Reese and Steinberg,

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.610
as well as the problems of this division.

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:32.280
Major composers that we recognize

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:35.460
like Victoria, Telles, Bird and Alan

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:38.300
are all extracurricular
to Reese's core language

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:41.737
despite their connections
however brief, to the center.

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:47.130
Chronologically the Renaissance
is also divided by Reese

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:48.380
into two further periods.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.380
The early Renaissance,
beginning in the early years

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:53.350
of the 15th century and
the late Renaissance

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:55.070
extending to the 16th century

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.820
and to the early years of the 17th.

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:00.720
Reese offers a substantial
often detailed discussion

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:02.080
of Clemens' music, more so

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:05.200
than any other Renaissance
music history to date,

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:07.030
but it's placing of Clemens in a long wake

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:09.870
of post Josquin followers,
created for the composer

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:12.950
a subordinate position
that has both endured

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:15.450
and shaped the historical
narrative of the period.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:19.800
In another much more recent
text on Renaissance music

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.150
by the Reese students, *Alan
Atlas, which is on the screen,

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:25.400
a remarkable book, this
is not on the screen.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:29.840
A remarkable book in many
ways that is full of insights.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:33.570
He even wonders whether we
should call Clemens' period,

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:35.033
the no name generation.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.010
One of the most eminent
musicologists of the period,

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:41.910
Richard Taruskin in a
rare act of compliance

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:44.430
with other existing musicological opinion,

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:47.893
also hangs Clemens in
the post-Josquin closet.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:53.040
Clemens illustrates how
the history, geography

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:54.530
and travel of Renaissance music

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:56.930
need to be scrutinized
and aligned more closely.

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:00.300
Clemens' life runs contrary
to what we've accepted

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:01.970
about Renaissance music history,

00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:03.670
the center, and the periphery,

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.830
the traditional patterns of
influence and transmission,

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:09.230
basically the conventional
geomatics of history.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.910
He did not travel far from his home

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.010
and he was not part of the
Burgundian Italian map,

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:17.620
he was therefore excluded
from consideration.

00:22:17.620 --> 00:22:20.550
He came after Josquin
and before Palestrina

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:23.170
and remained in an area that
was not deemed progressive

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:25.663
as the musicological
focus turned to Italy.

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:29.378
In order to show how inaccurate

00:22:29.378 --> 00:22:31.780
this assessment is of Clemens,

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:34.230
let us look at the wide
geographic dispersion

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:35.980
of one of his songs.

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:39.100
Not the song itself in its
original version for voices,

00:22:39.100 --> 00:22:40.850
but by the instrumental arrangements

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:44.113
of this piece made by other
musicians of the 16th century.

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.463
The first one appearing in print in 1545.

00:22:49.698 --> 00:22:52.400
The song appears first in 1545,

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.570
and the arrangements reveal the
long popularity of this work

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:57.700
and how this music got into the hands

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.800
of many need different types of players,

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:01.360
themselves creating arrangements

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.703
for yet other audiences throughout Europe.

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.170
This is from a manuscript in *Grand pres

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:12.790
which contains chanson, French chansons

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:14.540
by a number of composers.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:18.523
You see *Gisa Lamb here
and, Claude and Richafort,

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:22.080
and then right in the box
you'll see Clemens non Papa.

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:26.430
Entitled Je prens en Gré

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.180
the song is one of his most famous.

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:07.480
The beautiful handling of texts,

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:10.490
and the syntactical
connections between texts

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:12.760
rhythms and musical rhythms.

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:15.234
It's not surprising that Je prens en Gré,

00:24:15.234 --> 00:24:17.770
first published in 1543,

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:19.620
durably survived in the Hip Parade

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.003
for another half century,

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:24.940
due in large part to the many arrangements

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:26.770
that were made in French tablature,

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:28.847
Italian tablature, German lute tablature,

00:24:28.847 --> 00:24:31.950
*Citrin tablature and for keyboard, harp,

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:33.500
organ and viola.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:38.360
This on the screen is the
first lute arrangement

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:43.360
of this piece "Je prens
en gré" from Leuven

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:47.713
and it's an arrangement
in French lute tablature.

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:53.920
These instrumental versions
are essentially translations

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.520
of an original text or translations

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.640
of an original musical text
and like any translation

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:01.940
they point at new target audiences,

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.610
they arrive at new
geographical destinations.

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:07.350
And because these arrangements
are purely instrumental,

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:10.280
that is they expunge the
original French text.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.833
They are able to cross
cultural and linguistic divides

00:25:12.833 --> 00:25:14.193
throughout Europe.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:19.620
Here's a transmission of
this song Je prends en gré

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:22.060
using the same map.

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:26.270
The original was published
in 1539 as a vocal piece,

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:27.230
but by 1547

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:35.580
we get this first publication for lute,

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:39.350
and the publisher,* Fales
not only publishes in 1547,

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:42.920
he publishes it again in 52 and
then nine years later in 63.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:45.280
And then again in 71, and again in 82,

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:47.960
basically every 10 years, he's
coming out with this song.

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:49.783
This gives you an idea of the demand

00:25:49.783 --> 00:25:54.783
that Fales thought this
song had in his time.

00:25:58.097 --> 00:26:03.097
In 1547, we find a version
coming out in Trento in Italy

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:07.243
by another lutenist named Simon Ginskev.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:11.630
1556, this song is published in Frankfurt,

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:14.883
in 1557 in Jaén in Spain.

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:19.690
1562 in Trieste by another Lutenist,

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:22.930
a blind lutenist from from Pulia

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:27.413
who settled in Trieste
named* Japlo Gorzanis.

00:26:27.413 --> 00:26:29.650
In 1572 Strasbourg is published.

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:31.860
1576 back to Spain in Valladolid

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:36.627
1577, surprisingly it took a long time

00:26:36.627 --> 00:26:40.020
for it to get to 1577 to
be published in Venice,

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:42.180
mainly because Venice's printing presses

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.683
were mostly publishing Italian madrigals.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:50.610
In 1578 we have it published
for a Spanish lute in Madrid

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.490
and 1583 we have the last version

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:57.930
in the 16th century printed,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.550
not even talking about
what might have existed

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:01.363
in manuscript, in Nuremberg.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.790
Even though all these
instrumental arrangements

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:08.410
are different ranging
from highly ornamented

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.230
and flamboyant designs
to literal reproductions

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:13.483
of the musical texts' original song.

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.130
These versions dispel the
standard musicological notion

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:19.240
that Clemens's original
vocal piece in French

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:21.170
is the central source.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.970
With these instrumental versions,

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.600
most of them made years
after Clemens' death

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:28.653
toiling in peripheral
outposts *off the main grid.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.120
On the contrary, these arrangements map

00:27:32.120 --> 00:27:35.960
a wide polycentric
geography of Clemens' song.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.870
One that allows for a
multifocal and egalitarian view

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:41.193
of the work across
international boundaries.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.790
They extend the distance
that a word can travel

00:27:44.790 --> 00:27:46.770
from its point of origin,

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:48.740
from its composition as a vocal piece

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:51.340
to a transcription into
other regional notations,

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:53.640
leading to an adaptation
of the original work,

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:55.450
and finally a new performance

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:57.450
destined for different audiences

00:27:57.450 --> 00:27:59.423
in diverse geographical locations.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:03.163
To conclude this first case study,

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:06.040
confronting Clemens' brilliant music

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.200
from various musicological,
literary, historical

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:11.970
geographical and even
performance perspectives

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:16.120
forces us to question the
hierarchy and priority

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:18.960
that underlies the conventional
geographic boundaries

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:20.113
of Renaissance music.

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:24.460
It offers the chance to
scrutinize such notions

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:27.990
as so-called central and
peripheral traditions

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:29.520
and source types.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:32.120
Greater and lesser
composers quote unquote,

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:34.710
singular versus plural
modes of performance

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:38.060
and the relationship
between original works

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:39.160
and their adaptations.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:47.410
This leads me directly
into my second case study,

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:49.340
which I've already hinted at,

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:51.950
that is one major reason
for the exclusion of Clemens

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:53.560
from the narrative of Renaissance music.

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.610
Its that instrumental
arrangements of his work,

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:58.320
no matter how clearly they reveal

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:01.490
the wide international
dissemination of music,

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:05.290
as we have just seen, simply don't count.

00:29:05.290 --> 00:29:08.380
Instrumental adaptations expand the map,

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:09.740
but because instrumental music

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:12.840
was a transitory and
highly volatile repertory,

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:15.820
much of an improvise,
before the 16th century

00:29:15.820 --> 00:29:19.210
virtually all of it was either
improvised or memorized,

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:22.223
led to its neglect in modern
historical scholarship.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:27.220
Primacy has always been
given to notated vocal music,

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:29.310
but this neglect would've
seemed unthinkable

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:32.440
for those who experienced
the soundscape of the time.

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:34.580
The reality is that any
history of this music

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:36.140
will need to develop a wide angle

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:39.810
rather than the narrowly focused
view we have used until now

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:41.940
to include the contributions of players

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:44.860
that were neither committed
exclusively to notation

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:47.270
nor bound to an authorized style of text

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:48.483
or style of performance.

00:29:49.610 --> 00:29:51.860
Renaissance instrumental
music moves across

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:54.560
international and linguistic boundaries.

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:56.807
It is the product of both
autonomous composition

00:29:56.807 --> 00:29:58.920
and arrangements of other pieces

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:01.553
often resulting in many
versions of the same work.

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:04.730
At the textual level,
the derivative versions

00:30:04.730 --> 00:30:06.800
made by many types of musicians,

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.240
confront the critical
and philological methods

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:10.950
that have generally blueprinted

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:14.020
how Renaissance music
history has been studied.

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:15.540
Thus in examining this music,

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:17.150
one must resist the temptation

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:20.560
to prioritize pieces
and particular versions

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:23.270
since so many of them
are tightly interwoven

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:25.733
with culture and function across class.

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:29.470
We must remember how
much of the vocal music

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.820
of this period was arranged
by instrumentalists

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:35.400
and popularized and notated
instrumental versions.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:38.080
For many listeners of the Renaissance,

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:41.150
these instrumental arrangements
were both the initial

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:42.800
and most frequent contact

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:45.210
of say Josquin's music much more

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.100
than for those who heard his music

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:49.913
during restricted services
in courtly chapels.

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:53.890
In addition to their use of
many well-known dance tunes

00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.450
or songs and their
frequent roles as musicians

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:57.970
hired by the city,

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:01.330
instrumentalists also
played a practical role

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:03.000
in the geography of the city.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:04.350
Trumpets sounding their instruments.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.540
when the gates of the
city opened and closed.

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:08.220
Playing for civic processions,

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.563
announcing visitors and the like.

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:16.730
Returning to the Steinberg drawing,

00:31:16.730 --> 00:31:18.990
it shows the traditional perspective

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:20.490
of Renaissance scholarship,

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.700
where the foreground
represents vocal music

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:24.900
cultivated at the institutional level,

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:26.580
whether court or cathedral

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:29.410
followed by everything
else in the distance.

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:30.860
Instrumental music permits us to create

00:31:30.860 --> 00:31:32.400
a more detailed polycentric map

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:34.910
of Renaissance music so
that we can see exactly

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:36.573
what is on the other side.

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:40.050
We are aware of the enormous expenses

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:42.450
courtly chapels needed
to maintain a choir,

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:45.590
a scriptorium, and for
the training of musicians.

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:48.220
What about the music that
was heard everywhere else?

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:50.330
Nuremberg and Augsburg,

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:52.560
the largest most important
cities in Southern Germany,

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.320
but only half the size of say Bologna,

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:57.740
supported groups of maybe four musicians

00:31:57.740 --> 00:31:59.140
who played for the city.

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:01.470
A reminder that the urban centers of Italy

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:04.100
were consistently much larger
than those of the north

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:05.993
and replete with funding for music.

00:32:07.694 --> 00:32:09.930
Although the bulk of
archival data about musicians

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:12.750
is concerned mostly with
details of compensation

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:15.160
rather than what they actually played.

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.770
It remains difficult to produce
anything like a pie chart,

00:32:17.770 --> 00:32:20.620
showing the relative expenditures
of a town like Nuremberg

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:21.453
on music.

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:25.720
However, for a small city like Wintzenheim

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:29.170
we can estimate that instrumental
music absorbed nearly 20%

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:31.730
of the city's disposable yearly budget,

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:32.830
which is considerable.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:35.850
These smaller cities also tell us

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:37.730
about the social rank of musicians,

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:40.660
their origins, and their
personal geography.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:43.530
For example, many musicians
from Augsburg and Nuremberg

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.540
found lucrative careers
in Florence and Ferrara.

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:48.940
Some came from very small villages,

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:53.360
the records of Augsburg
between 1450 and 1500,

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:55.780
indicate that several
musicians engaged by the city

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:57.580
during that time were from places

00:32:57.580 --> 00:32:59.730
that must have had tiny populations,

00:32:59.730 --> 00:33:01.883
200 or less in several cases.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.829
Schrattenbach, remarkable cases

00:33:06.829 --> 00:33:09.270
that have the minuscule
lower Bavarian town

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.520
of Schrattenbach near Augsburg,

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:15.750
which has a modern day
population of only just over 300.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.713
It was probably distinctly
smaller in the 15th century.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:21.790
This was the home of the *Ralph family

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:24.140
of musicians and instrument banquets.

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:27.770
In 1473, *Klaus Ralph
provided the city of Augsburg

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:29.600
with three pipers.

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:32.440
At this time Schrattenbach
was such an obscure place

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.550
that the Augsburg accountant inscribes,

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:37.840
felt it necessary to take
the extremely unusual step

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:39.470
of describing the location

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:41.610
in relation to its neighboring city,

00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:43.177
when they listed the payment,

00:33:43.177 --> 00:33:47.070
*paid four golden to RA in Schrattenbach

00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:48.660
which is two miles from Kempten

00:33:49.619 --> 00:33:51.420
and Kempten itself was one of the smallest

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:52.470
of the Imperial city.

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.460
These are just a few entertaining examples

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:57.860
from the archives, but the
point is that instrumental music

00:33:57.860 --> 00:33:59.610
provides a more detailed, inclusive,

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:03.007
and frankly more accurate
modern map of Renaissance music

00:34:03.007 --> 00:34:05.980
in which roads clearly
lead to other roads.

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:08.300
And we see a more detailed
network of influences,

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.900
backgrounds, and types
of musicians, context,

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:14.033
even the artisanal
infrastructure of music.

00:34:16.610 --> 00:34:20.610
My final case study, the ideal goal

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.590
of arriving at a polycentric
map of Renaissance music

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:25.330
involves a current editorial project,

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:28.640
about the most important
lutenist of the 16th century,

00:34:28.640 --> 00:34:33.220
Francesco da Milano, one
of the many lutenists

00:34:33.220 --> 00:34:35.280
whose birthplace on the musical map

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:37.600
is embedded in his professional name,

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:40.450
Francesco Canova of Milan.

00:34:40.450 --> 00:34:43.340
Similarly, there is Marco of Aquila,

00:34:43.340 --> 00:34:46.800
Alberto of Ripa, Perino the Florentine,

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:50.570
Giovanni Maria or Crema, Lorenzo of Pavia,

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:52.853
Anibale the Padawan and so on.

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:59.360
Here, I will briefly introduce
how the polycentric model

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:03.440
which does not subordinate
the periphery to the center

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:05.870
can incorporate the
individual, the regional,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:10.133
and even the idiosyncrasies a
performance into the addition.

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:13.740
Francesco the only musician

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:17.637
to share Michelangelo's epithet
of the divine, in divinum

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:20.570
I guess Bette Midler would be the third

00:35:20.570 --> 00:35:23.090
was one of the most
highly esteemed musicians

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:25.847
of the 16th century and
the most influential

00:35:25.847 --> 00:35:27.947
and important lutenist of the Renaissance.

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:30.350
Flourishing during a period

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:32.900
when the musical establishments
of most Italian courts

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.680
were dominated by northerners.

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:36.550
As we have noted earlier,

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:38.940
Francesco was the first
Italian born musician

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:42.173
of the Renaissance to achieve
truly international fame.

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:45.260
His music circulated widely in Europe

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:47.690
through prints anthologies
and manuscripts.

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:49.670
And by the beginning of the 17th century,

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:52.210
his works had achieved a classic sense

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:54.980
withstanding the dramatic
changes in musical style

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.430
and modifications to the instrument itself

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:58.263
that had rendered

00:35:58.263 --> 00:36:00.923
most of the 16th century
lute repertory obsolete.

00:36:02.230 --> 00:36:04.550
His music continued to appear in English

00:36:04.550 --> 00:36:07.593
and continental sources,
a century after his death.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.970
Making an addition of Francesco's music,

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:13.690
and this would be the second,

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:17.220
is notoriously complicated
because of the plurality

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:20.880
of source types and different
regional types of notation.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:25.060
Authorized prints, anthologies,
student pedagogical books,

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:28.500
Florentine sources, Neapolitan
sources, Roman sources,

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:30.740
English sources, postumus sources,

00:36:30.740 --> 00:36:33.370
incomplete sources, unreadable sources,

00:36:33.370 --> 00:36:36.347
pieces copied from a print,
pieces copied from memory,

00:36:36.347 --> 00:36:39.313
truncated pieces, ornamented pieces,

00:36:42.531 --> 00:36:44.283
pieces clearly not by Francesco
but attributed to him.

00:36:45.490 --> 00:36:47.330
Anonymous pieces that nevertheless

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:49.820
pass the test stylistcally,

00:36:49.820 --> 00:36:52.730
to attribute them to Francesco, et cetera.

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:54.830
The sheer number of
versions that exist for us,

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:58.420
just a single piece can introduce
dozens of complex issues

00:36:58.420 --> 00:37:00.120
regarding authenticity,

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:02.050
particularly among the 70 or so pieces

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:03.550
that appeared after his death.

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:06.900
And of the pieces for which
we are relatively secure

00:37:06.900 --> 00:37:10.520
by Francesco, which version does one use?

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:12.180
The Neapolitan version that contains

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:15.660
lengthy ornamented passages
and unusual dissonances

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:17.947
or the more conservative,
but also more balanced,

00:37:17.947 --> 00:37:18.947
*Indonesian version.

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:24.160
An attempt at a complete
addition was completed in 1970,

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:28.130
which resulted in
approximately 125 compositions

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:31.740
attributed to Francesco,
of which only 51 pieces

00:37:31.740 --> 00:37:33.940
appeared during his lifetime.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:36.200
Without reviewing the
merits, but more importantly,

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:37.740
the deficiencies of this addition

00:37:37.740 --> 00:37:40.220
that have made it
imperative to do a new one,

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.340
suffice it to say that it was a product

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:44.440
of the rigid editorial
philosophy at Harvard

00:37:44.440 --> 00:37:46.290
during the late 1960s,

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.990
in which choices about what
version to include or exclude

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:50.240
in an addition were made

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:52.730
by adopting the central
peripheral template.

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:55.220
Central sources being
those that were printed,

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:57.600
even if they contained works
of dubious authorships,

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:01.120
and there were many, and
manuscripts of elite pedigree

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.920
generally copied by a single scribe.

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:06.570
Peripheral ones like
those copied by students,

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:09.430
17th century sources and
prints like the Neapolitan one

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:12.900
I just described were not used

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:15.123
even if they contained superior readings.

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:19.570
In this addition, I will
employ a polycentric approach,

00:38:19.570 --> 00:38:21.290
essentially advocating for the use

00:38:21.290 --> 00:38:23.600
of many different types of sources

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:25.780
and benefiting from the
digital format of the addition

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:27.520
that will allow this.

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:29.210
When pieces have multiple versions,

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:31.120
that contain significant variants,

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:33.396
they will all be included
as a way to redraw

00:38:33.396 --> 00:38:36.590
and expand the map to include

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:40.210
who actually played Francesco's music,

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:41.310
and to avoid the trappings

00:38:41.310 --> 00:38:44.533
of the traditional center
and periphery model.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:47.440
This means that not only are cultural,

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:50.300
regional, chronological
and textual differences

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:51.853
validated by their inclusion,

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:55.650
the variance documents
something that I believe

00:38:55.650 --> 00:38:57.420
is critical to this project.

00:38:57.420 --> 00:39:00.010
They reveal different
performances of the work

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:03.600
that took place over time and space

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.010
and how players incorporated

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:07.970
their individual musical
and technical abilities

00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:09.923
across a wide geographical spectrum.

00:39:12.340 --> 00:39:14.993
I thought we would hear
one piece by Francesco,

00:39:15.905 --> 00:39:19.543
if this was live I was going
to come and play a piece.

00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:23.230
I do have a recording of Francesco,

00:39:23.230 --> 00:39:24.923
from a source that was used.

00:39:25.900 --> 00:39:30.470
It's an important source that's
called the Siena lute book,

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:32.623
even though it is now housed in the Hague.

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:35.480
It's a manuscript version,
manuscript source,

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:37.599
and it contains one of Francesco's,

00:39:37.599 --> 00:39:41.359
(speaks in foreign language)

00:39:41.359 --> 00:39:42.780
So this is a recording I made,

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:45.113
I don't know, 15, 20 years ago, I think.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:48.937
This is in Italian tablature

00:39:48.937 --> 00:39:51.900
and I don't know how many
people read Italian tablature.

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:54.600
So I'll just use my cursor
as kind of the bouncing ball,

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:56.730
as we hear this piece.

00:39:56.730 --> 00:39:58.840
It goes from here, all the way to here.

00:40:00.707 --> 00:40:03.540
(soft lute music)

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:10.400
So in conclusion,

00:43:10.400 --> 00:43:12.750
the active creating an
inclusive and connective network

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.160
of all musical practices,

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:16.900
sung, played, written and unwritten,

00:43:16.900 --> 00:43:19.650
quickly reveals flaws in the
hierarchies and priorities

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:21.410
that have largely mapped the geography

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:23.670
of early modern music history.

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:26.490
But such *churny can also
offer the chance for revision

00:43:26.490 --> 00:43:29.110
that is to move across a
historically constructed

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:30.560
and still not outdated

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:32.760
boundaries of central
and peripheral styles,

00:43:34.460 --> 00:43:36.070
greater and lesser composers,

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:38.110
singular and plural modes of performance

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:40.330
notated and improvised practices

00:43:40.330 --> 00:43:42.810
and the border between
original compositions

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:45.057
and their adaptations. Thank you.

00:43:48.246 --> 00:43:51.753
- Thank you so much Victor
for the wonderful talk,

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:58.190
showing us how the sort of
disrupting this sort of dichotomy

00:43:58.190 --> 00:44:01.130
of the center and the periphery,

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:06.130
and revealing to us that
geography has played

00:44:06.290 --> 00:44:09.000
a really important role

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:12.380
in establishing the notion of tradition.

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:15.883
So if I may start with the first question.

00:44:17.180 --> 00:44:19.160
I was curious with,

00:44:19.160 --> 00:44:23.230
your three case studies
were really fascinating,

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:26.070
because of how, in the
case of Francesco da Milano

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:28.010
and the other, Marco da Aquila,

00:44:28.010 --> 00:44:32.462
you showed how place is very
much part of the identity.

00:44:32.462 --> 00:44:33.651
- Yeah

00:44:33.651 --> 00:44:37.700
- And it also potentially,

00:44:37.700 --> 00:44:42.700
I gather, sort of informs style
and even notation perhaps,

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:46.470
sort of a uniformity.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:49.630
So I was curious, is there any kind of,

00:44:49.630 --> 00:44:52.390
given that there are instrumental
modifications over time

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:54.570
and changes in notation,

00:44:54.570 --> 00:44:57.660
is there any kind of universalism at all,

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.513
or does it not really
make sense to talk about,

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:06.970
sort of core ways in which
you could make connections

00:45:06.970 --> 00:45:09.920
across time and across place?

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:10.770
- That's a good question.

00:45:10.770 --> 00:45:13.830
I mean, there does seem to be
a kind of notion of progress

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:17.163
that takes place certainly
in certain centers in Italy.

00:45:18.911 --> 00:45:22.160
Perhaps one model for a universalism

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:24.410
would be, towards the end of the century

00:45:24.410 --> 00:45:29.410
when printers begin to
put together anthologies

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:32.540
and the anthology begins to then

00:45:32.540 --> 00:45:35.680
bring together works that were popular,

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:40.680
that had circulated that
were clearly consumed

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:44.053
at many, many different levels.

00:45:44.910 --> 00:45:46.790
These anthologies were very successful

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:49.520
when they came out and I
wonder whether that might be

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:51.352
where a lot of these composers

00:45:51.352 --> 00:45:55.410
received sort of classic
status by being anthologized

00:45:55.410 --> 00:46:00.250
similar to literature
in how poets and writers

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:02.570
become anthologized, those anthologies

00:46:02.570 --> 00:46:06.600
become standard reading at institutions

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:10.510
and pretty soon that kind
of American literature

00:46:10.510 --> 00:46:13.630
comes into focus, Northern
literature comes into focus

00:46:13.630 --> 00:46:15.730
and that's how those things become mapped.

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:19.350
We don't pay enough attention
I think, to anthologies

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:21.410
but that I think would be one place

00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:22.800
where I would start with this.

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:24.830
And they're both manuscript anthologies

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:27.160
and printed anthologies.

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:28.770
Manuscript ones are even more fascinating

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:31.760
because now we see a
person, an actual person.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:33.140
In this case, the person, one of them

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:36.170
that I've studied as a young
17 year old Florentine,

00:46:36.170 --> 00:46:38.090
who puts in his manuscript works

00:46:38.090 --> 00:46:39.530
from the early 16th century

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:41.640
all the way to pieces of his time.

00:46:41.640 --> 00:46:43.540
Works to sing, works to play,

00:46:43.540 --> 00:46:44.880
works to play with his friends.

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:47.440
I mean, there's a very interesting aspect

00:46:47.440 --> 00:46:50.613
of here's my personal
repertoire, here's my playlist.

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:56.380
This is my playlist and
playlists are a good analogy

00:46:56.380 --> 00:47:00.163
because playlists do not
sit in one period of time,

00:47:01.562 --> 00:47:02.930
they come from various places.

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:05.910
they're suggested, people are tagging it,

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.790
they're coming in, in
so many different ways.

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:11.500
And your playlist grows in
a very interesting fashion.

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:13.640
These magic mythologies are in a case

00:47:13.640 --> 00:47:14.840
somewhat the same thing.

00:47:21.200 --> 00:47:22.100
- That's intriguing, right?

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:25.690
That the anthologies might be
the place to kind of track.

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:29.297
So I had another question
about sort of the center

00:47:29.297 --> 00:47:31.643
and the periphery right and I wonder,

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:38.520
you did not talk about any women composers

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:40.593
or women performers.

00:47:42.410 --> 00:47:44.430
Are they just completely absent

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:45.950
from the sort of written record

00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:47.520
though they existed, obviously?

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:50.600
- No, not at all in fact,
during the past few weeks,

00:47:50.600 --> 00:47:52.840
we've learned about some major discoveries

00:47:52.840 --> 00:47:54.570
by musicologist Lori Strauss

00:47:54.570 --> 00:47:58.137
of a whole book of madrigals,

00:47:59.260 --> 00:48:03.310
by a very well known or now
well known female composer,

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:04.580
and there are madrigal composers.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:06.807
The problem, there were
lots of female lutenists

00:48:06.807 --> 00:48:08.970
and in the book that I
wrote with Keith Polk,

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:13.970
we devote some time to
understanding the issue of gender

00:48:14.326 --> 00:48:16.180
in terms of performance,

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:20.370
but professional roots
were pretty much blocked

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:24.280
where female musicians
tend to begin to thrive

00:48:24.280 --> 00:48:26.230
in the courts of Mantua and Ferrara

00:48:26.230 --> 00:48:28.520
during the late 16th century

00:48:29.808 --> 00:48:33.410
as madrigal singers,
virtuoso madrigal singers

00:48:34.550 --> 00:48:36.740
that put them on the doorstep to opera

00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:38.253
in the 17th century.

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:43.810
We know about Isabella d'Este
and other women of means

00:48:43.810 --> 00:48:45.550
who played the lute and composed music.

00:48:45.550 --> 00:48:47.440
I don't think that I've ever found though,

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:49.750
a piece that could be securely attributed

00:48:49.750 --> 00:48:51.213
to a female lutenist.

00:48:52.369 --> 00:48:55.630
- So it was not that the women

00:48:55.630 --> 00:48:57.520
were potentially part of the periphery.

00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:01.960
We're still talking about like a very,

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:05.023
male composer, performer,

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:08.810
- Yeah and the repertories
associated with them, you know,

00:49:08.810 --> 00:49:12.033
courtly repertories versus
non-courtly repertories.

00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:16.990
Other than a few people

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:19.500
that I think were on the zoom call,

00:49:19.500 --> 00:49:23.723
music for civic uses, music in cities,

00:49:24.870 --> 00:49:27.370
was not very much studied,
a lot of that is improvised,

00:49:27.370 --> 00:49:28.550
a lot of that is arranged.

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:32.550
And when you bring in
improvisation and arrangement,

00:49:32.550 --> 00:49:34.760
those two verbs are fighting words

00:49:34.760 --> 00:49:38.840
for many people because they
indicate a type of music

00:49:38.840 --> 00:49:42.300
that was what I call
evanescent that's fleeting.

00:49:42.300 --> 00:49:47.090
It's not permanent and arrangements

00:49:47.090 --> 00:49:51.560
tend to be considered as corruptions of

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:57.250
or text that takes it out
of the composer's hands.

00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:59.140
But there's no question
that composers understood

00:49:59.140 --> 00:50:01.240
that their music was going to be arranged.

00:50:02.610 --> 00:50:04.990
There are multiple versions
of their vocal music anyway.

00:50:04.990 --> 00:50:08.600
So I think the periphery
is quite complicated,

00:50:08.600 --> 00:50:12.240
it's both rural, it's classes of musicians

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:14.390
who are not elite musicians,

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:16.650
they come from families

00:50:16.650 --> 00:50:20.190
that have been trumpeters
for many generations

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:23.150
or lutenists, they're lute makers,

00:50:23.150 --> 00:50:26.260
there's a huge infrastructure
of instrument makers

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:28.485
that we often don't consider that

00:50:28.485 --> 00:50:29.833
especially in Venice,

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:33.430
The periphery contains,

00:50:33.430 --> 00:50:34.313
well what used to be called a periphery,

00:50:34.313 --> 00:50:35.710
(Victor mumbles)

00:50:35.710 --> 00:50:37.978
contains all of that material.

00:50:37.978 --> 00:50:39.670
Instrumental music in general

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:44.670
has been relegated to that in many ways.

00:50:50.440 --> 00:50:51.440
- Yeah, go ahead Ty

00:50:52.646 --> 00:50:54.203
- I had a question if I may.

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:58.010
I wanted, and please I'm
showing my ignorance here,

00:50:58.010 --> 00:51:01.790
I had studied flute and
piccolo classically for years,

00:51:01.790 --> 00:51:04.400
but I am ignorant of tablature

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:07.300
as mentioned specifically
Italian tablature

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:08.133
so forgive me.

00:51:08.133 --> 00:51:10.140
Would you explain it a bit more?

00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:12.520
It seems to be based more
on a numbering system,

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:15.200
I'm used to more traditional notes.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:20.200
- Yeah I mean, we still
use tablature all the time,

00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:22.820
if you go on the internet and look for

00:51:24.770 --> 00:51:26.760
the tablatures to Hotel California,

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:29.883
you'll get 20 of them all wrong.

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:35.200
So let's put up the *Fales here.

00:51:35.200 --> 00:51:36.070
- Yeah, that'd be great.

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:38.364
- This would be the clearest one.

00:51:38.364 --> 00:51:39.860
- Wondering about that.

00:51:39.860 --> 00:51:41.770
- So this is called French tablature

00:51:41.770 --> 00:51:45.180
and the lines that go across
from from left to right

00:51:45.180 --> 00:51:47.280
are not staff lines those
are the strings of the lute,

00:51:47.280 --> 00:51:49.410
one, two, three, four, five,

00:51:49.410 --> 00:51:52.490
there's five here, and then he'll put,

00:51:52.490 --> 00:51:53.480
there are six strings on the lute,

00:51:53.480 --> 00:51:55.490
so he has to put the letters,

00:51:55.490 --> 00:51:58.460
for the bottom string below the staff,

00:51:58.460 --> 00:52:00.260
you can see where my cursor is here.

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:03.270
Okay, so this is the
top string of the lute,

00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:06.620
a G, the second string a D here's an A,

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:09.410
there's an F there's a C, and
then there's a G down here,

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:10.710
which he doesn't indicate

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:14.275
mainly because he used
this font and he used this,

00:52:14.275 --> 00:52:16.820
you know the blocks are
printing for many other uses.

00:52:16.820 --> 00:52:17.970
I don't think he had a template

00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:19.290
for six lines of tablature

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:23.900
so he just did five and
figured out the rest.

00:52:23.900 --> 00:52:26.200
The letters indicate
where you put your fingers

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:27.193
on the frets.

00:52:28.419 --> 00:52:30.670
So A means that you play the string open,

00:52:30.670 --> 00:52:33.970
you don't have to fret anything
it's just an open string.

00:52:33.970 --> 00:52:36.440
B means you put it on the first fret,

00:52:36.440 --> 00:52:39.180
C means second fret D third, et cetera.

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:41.360
So when the letters are all piled up

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:43.290
one on top of each other, that's a cord.

00:52:43.290 --> 00:52:46.360
That means that that
fourth string is open.

00:52:46.360 --> 00:52:48.310
That D would be the third fret,

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:49.780
then you put your finger on the third fret

00:52:49.780 --> 00:52:51.110
of the third string,

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:52.950
and you put your finger on the third fret

00:52:52.950 --> 00:52:56.350
of the second string, and
you get actually an F chord.

00:52:56.350 --> 00:52:58.060
And then he puts the rhythms up here.

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.010
Now the rhythms only give indication

00:53:00.010 --> 00:53:03.160
of the fastest voice at any one time.

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:06.530
And so, right here, the
fastest voice at any one time

00:53:06.530 --> 00:53:08.930
is what's going on in the top string.

00:53:08.930 --> 00:53:12.813
And in other places like in
the piece that I played later,

00:53:13.810 --> 00:53:14.663
see here.

00:53:18.200 --> 00:53:23.200
Sometimes the fastest
notes are on the bottom.

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:25.531
So this is Italian tablature.

00:53:25.531 --> 00:53:27.070
Italian tablature reverses everything

00:53:27.070 --> 00:53:27.903
now there's no,

00:53:27.903 --> 00:53:29.990
- Yeah. I was gonna ask about
the differences thank you.

00:53:29.990 --> 00:53:32.720
- There's no letters now there's numbers.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:36.510
- So now the A that we saw in
French tablature becomes zero.

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:39.347
So zero is the open string,
and one is the first fret.

00:53:39.347 --> 00:53:40.740
The second is the second fret,

00:53:40.740 --> 00:53:42.140
that makes a little bit more sense,

00:53:42.140 --> 00:53:43.470
but that's not the only difference.

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:47.590
Now, the first string is
down here, the highest string

00:53:47.590 --> 00:53:50.773
and the lowest string is up
here, so they reverse that.

00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:55.230
So you have to kind of
learn both of these topics.

00:53:55.230 --> 00:53:56.063
Then there's German tablature,

00:53:56.063 --> 00:54:00.670
which assigns a unique
letter to every single fret

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:03.230
in every single note, typical.

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:05.500
So here's a good example
of the lowest voice

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:07.400
having all the rhythms.

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:08.520
So you just put the rhythm there

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:09.570
and you play all that stuff,

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:11.783
and then it goes to the top.

00:54:11.783 --> 00:54:14.010
It's a visual depiction really,

00:54:14.010 --> 00:54:15.810
of where you put your fingers

00:54:15.810 --> 00:54:19.810
and the irony is that
tablature was invented

00:54:19.810 --> 00:54:21.000
to make things easy

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:23.460
so people did not have
to read staff notation,

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:26.840
but it's exactly what has
prevented this repertory

00:54:26.840 --> 00:54:30.213
from being widely known in
the musicological literature.

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:34.650
- And composers, Renaissance
composers in particular,

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:38.210
would've been conversant in
German, French and Italian.

00:54:38.210 --> 00:54:41.360
- No, I don't think
anybody outside of Germany

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:42.713
was conversant in German.

00:54:44.950 --> 00:54:48.260
That's why German tablature
faded by the early 17th century.

00:54:48.260 --> 00:54:50.820
And what was left in the end?

00:54:50.820 --> 00:54:53.380
Italian tablature kind of
fades in the early 18th,

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:56.813
what was left was French,
It was French in the end.

00:54:58.770 --> 00:55:01.283
Very good question, those are good ones.

00:55:02.620 --> 00:55:05.653
- So I actually have
another question, which is,

00:55:07.883 --> 00:55:10.640
so Victor, when really does

00:55:11.524 --> 00:55:15.720
the pattern of patronage
say for lute music

00:55:15.720 --> 00:55:20.720
change from the court
and the church to more

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:24.540
music for the masses, or
was that always there?

00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:27.173
- I don't think lutes
were music for the masses,

00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:30.660
it's still the 1%.

00:55:31.940 --> 00:55:35.053
Or the middle class by the 16th century.

00:55:38.620 --> 00:55:41.510
The shift from the lute
to the Spanish guitar

00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:43.300
there, you're getting,
then they're saying though,

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:46.120
all the barbers play them and
it becomes very easy to play.

00:55:46.120 --> 00:55:48.470
And then you get a huge, huge difference

00:55:48.470 --> 00:55:52.120
in terms of class of players.

00:55:52.120 --> 00:55:54.200
But that takes us to
the early 17th century

00:55:54.200 --> 00:55:56.100
and that's taking place in Spain

00:55:56.100 --> 00:55:57.500
and in other parts of Italy.

00:55:59.760 --> 00:56:03.963
But the lute remains, a
noble courtly instrument.

00:56:05.889 --> 00:56:09.870
it remains a thing for
well educated youth.

00:56:11.560 --> 00:56:12.760
It doesn't seem to have had

00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:17.760
a kind of association with brothels

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:21.013
or the kinds of things that we see

00:56:21.013 --> 00:56:22.950
with the guitar and other instruments.

00:56:22.950 --> 00:56:25.630
You know, you look at
all the broil paintings,

00:56:25.630 --> 00:56:26.463
you'll never see a lute

00:56:26.463 --> 00:56:29.307
you'll see bagpipes and
you'll see things like that.

00:56:34.180 --> 00:56:35.480
- Since you are interested

00:56:35.480 --> 00:56:38.423
in cross-cultural musical influences,

00:56:40.100 --> 00:56:42.730
are there versions of the lute

00:56:42.730 --> 00:56:44.513
that are played around the world?

00:56:48.039 --> 00:56:49.490
- So that's where it changes

00:56:49.490 --> 00:56:51.683
and that's another problem
about the center periphery

00:56:51.683 --> 00:56:54.220
it's that, when I was a graduate student,

00:56:54.220 --> 00:56:55.700
you would've committed academic suicide

00:56:55.700 --> 00:56:59.480
by saying that you wanna
study new world music

00:56:59.480 --> 00:57:03.490
that was exported from
Spain or what I did in Goa

00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:04.740
and looking at Portuguese music

00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:06.963
that ended up in Goa India.

00:57:07.840 --> 00:57:10.140
But that is a huge, huge

00:57:10.140 --> 00:57:12.130
trove of information that's there.

00:57:12.130 --> 00:57:16.090
And the first boats that came
to India carried instruments,

00:57:16.090 --> 00:57:17.963
they had small portable organs.

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:23.593
That's what led these to
the harmonium in the end,

00:57:27.239 --> 00:57:29.820
these organs that we call portative,

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:31.190
these tiny small little ones

00:57:31.190 --> 00:57:32.640
that you can place on a table, which are,

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:34.510
you can see in many medieval portraits,

00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:36.550
those are some of the
instruments that were used

00:57:36.550 --> 00:57:39.343
to accompany chant when
they had mass on the boats.

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:43.620
And when they came to India
and they set up churches,

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:45.300
they brought those instruments.

00:57:45.300 --> 00:57:47.550
Probably the English were the ones

00:57:47.550 --> 00:57:50.230
who were very influential

00:57:50.230 --> 00:57:55.230
in the organ becoming
associated with the harmonium.

00:57:56.200 --> 00:57:58.800
But the harmonium was
essentially a portative organ.

00:58:00.482 --> 00:58:03.710
That's probably one of the
only fixed pitch instruments

00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:04.543
in India.

00:58:04.543 --> 00:58:06.523
All the other ones are variable pitch.

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:14.940
- But in terms of motifs
and actual composition,

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:19.940
content of composition
and sort of structure

00:58:20.380 --> 00:58:23.433
are cross-cultural influences discernible?

00:58:30.544 --> 00:58:33.740
Did the mixture of cultures in Spain

00:58:33.740 --> 00:58:38.170
in the 12th, 13th, 14th
centuries with the *Moores,

00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:40.910
did that actually have
impact on these traditions?

00:58:40.910 --> 00:58:41.880
- Yeah, I think that,

00:58:41.880 --> 00:58:45.430
when you have, for example,
in the new world polyphony,

00:58:45.430 --> 00:58:47.210
what's interesting is
the choice of the texts,

00:58:47.210 --> 00:58:49.270
liturgical texts, that
they venerate saints,

00:58:49.270 --> 00:58:53.300
that were particularly
venerated among new Catholics,

00:58:53.300 --> 00:58:55.343
newly baptized Catholics, et cetera,

00:58:56.608 --> 00:58:57.441
those are the ones that are just,

00:58:57.441 --> 00:58:59.050
which are very different
from the types of saints

00:58:59.050 --> 00:59:01.860
that, you know, might have
written texts for in...

00:59:03.750 --> 00:59:07.059
- What about the sounds
themselves, the sounds themselves?

00:59:07.059 --> 00:59:11.110
- So in India, we have a few manuscripts,

00:59:11.110 --> 00:59:13.070
but they're all from convents.

00:59:13.070 --> 00:59:15.070
They're all written for very high voices

00:59:16.210 --> 00:59:19.920
and they tend to be all dedicated

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:21.520
to St. Michael the protector.

00:59:21.520 --> 00:59:25.963
So there's that,

00:59:27.330 --> 00:59:28.840
whether that lets us know

00:59:28.840 --> 00:59:32.050
about particularly indigenous qualities,

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:35.433
that it's not clear because
these are written texts.

00:59:37.010 --> 00:59:38.510
So the only thing would be

00:59:40.460 --> 00:59:43.293
the texts themselves, the high voices,

00:59:44.780 --> 00:59:45.980
because they're convents

00:59:48.010 --> 00:59:50.430
and we don't have any written record

00:59:50.430 --> 00:59:55.430
of the syncretic music that
was very much part of Goa,

00:59:56.760 --> 00:59:58.280
around the 1560s and 70s

00:59:58.280 --> 01:00:02.660
before the council of Trent

01:00:02.660 --> 01:00:07.660
and other bodies pretty
much shut that down,

01:00:08.110 --> 01:00:10.460
but there was a lot of
syncretic music making

01:00:10.460 --> 01:00:14.810
and the archive show
*Shahamelas which are shams

01:00:14.810 --> 01:00:19.220
violas, *darcos, or bowed
stringed instruments,

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:20.933
and instruments of the land,

01:00:22.269 --> 01:00:24.017
there was that as well.

01:00:24.017 --> 01:00:26.020
So there would've been, I think,

01:00:26.020 --> 01:00:28.570
an indigenous quality to music making

01:00:28.570 --> 01:00:30.510
within these institutions,

01:00:30.510 --> 01:00:34.533
but there's no musical record
of those, unfortunately.

01:00:38.720 --> 01:00:42.560
- So for example the
Indian *older traditions

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:43.713
musical traditions,

01:00:44.910 --> 01:00:49.200
seem to have appeared quite independently,

01:00:49.200 --> 01:00:53.670
so music and north Indian classical music.

01:00:53.670 --> 01:00:58.670
But the argument is made
that the forms of music,

01:00:58.810 --> 01:01:00.880
the forms of north Indian classical music,

01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:01.913
like the ghazal

01:01:02.981 --> 01:01:07.220
and these are very informed
by poetry and Persian.

01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:09.890
- But these are Urdu
texts that go very, very,

01:01:09.890 --> 01:01:12.783
very far back, yeah, exactly.

01:01:16.190 --> 01:01:17.640
The question I always
have with Indian music

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:20.960
is performance, you know,
does Harry *Patrasia,

01:01:20.960 --> 01:01:25.000
is he playing a style that
is 100 or 200 years old,

01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:27.370
or is he playing a style that has somehow

01:01:27.370 --> 01:01:29.493
been frozen from 400 years ago?

01:01:37.348 --> 01:01:39.470
- I think the argument is often made,

01:01:39.470 --> 01:01:41.440
when people talk about the difference

01:01:41.440 --> 01:01:43.900
between Indian classical
music and other traditions

01:01:43.900 --> 01:01:48.100
is that there isn't as
much room for improvisation

01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:50.970
in Indian traditions, right.

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:52.200
- We're in Indian traditions

01:01:52.200 --> 01:01:53.780
or outside of Indian traditions.

01:01:53.780 --> 01:01:56.063
- Outside of Indian traditions.

01:01:57.163 --> 01:01:58.840
- I've heard that in there

01:02:01.790 --> 01:02:04.080
that is one of the things
that has been eliminated

01:02:06.522 --> 01:02:09.380
from the writings about Renaissance music.

01:02:09.380 --> 01:02:11.980
How do you talk about 15th
century instrumental music?

01:02:11.980 --> 01:02:15.650
I mean, my colleague, Keith
Polk, I think he's on the call

01:02:15.650 --> 01:02:19.150
and I devote a fair amount of space

01:02:19.150 --> 01:02:20.523
in our most recent book.

01:02:21.430 --> 01:02:23.530
You have to work your way back sometimes.

01:02:23.530 --> 01:02:27.160
We have a source that might
reflect these traditions,

01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:28.960
but we have enough theoretical evidence

01:02:28.960 --> 01:02:31.470
about how they improvised and how they,

01:02:31.470 --> 01:02:34.200
you know, you went from
one voice or two voices,

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:36.690
to three voices, to
eventually four voices.

01:02:36.690 --> 01:02:41.020
Improvisation is a factor that's important

01:02:41.020 --> 01:02:43.370
all the way through the
end of the 18th century.

01:02:43.370 --> 01:02:47.901
And so while we recognize that now

01:02:47.901 --> 01:02:51.650
for performance of the 15th
and 16th centuries and 17th,

01:02:51.650 --> 01:02:55.290
we're now starting to recognize
that for more and more so

01:02:55.290 --> 01:02:58.160
for performance of the 18th
and 19th century music.

01:02:58.160 --> 01:02:59.700
But no, this new question on improvisation

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:03.933
is that blind spot in musicology,
Renaissance musicology.

01:03:06.100 --> 01:03:08.740
- Unrelated question
Victor, how illustrated

01:03:08.740 --> 01:03:11.700
were the tablature musical scores?

01:03:11.700 --> 01:03:15.040
There was an earlier, I
think the first musical piece

01:03:15.040 --> 01:03:19.040
he played, looked like it
had some illustrations on it.

01:03:19.040 --> 01:03:24.040
I mean were these richly
illustrated or not maybe?

01:03:24.270 --> 01:03:29.270
- Some were, the ones that were
illustrated were ones that,

01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:34.303
the owner wanted to keep for posterity.

01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:39.580
A good example is one that
I've just been working on,

01:03:39.580 --> 01:03:41.623
hold on, it says loading here,

01:03:44.406 --> 01:03:45.956
that I've just been working on.

01:03:55.070 --> 01:03:55.903
This manuscript

01:04:01.106 --> 01:04:02.870
this in the Newberry library,

01:04:02.870 --> 01:04:05.220
this is a manuscript
written by a Venetian,

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:08.100
a nobleman from *Dreshya

01:04:08.100 --> 01:04:11.003
and he had an artist
illustrate this manuscript.

01:04:12.248 --> 01:04:16.200
- So even if it was lost,
if it had become lost,

01:04:16.200 --> 01:04:18.193
somebody would keep it for posterity.

01:04:20.200 --> 01:04:21.300
- But this is unusual.

01:04:22.920 --> 01:04:25.890
Rarely are Renaissance lute manuscripts

01:04:25.890 --> 01:04:27.700
given to a professional copyist

01:04:27.700 --> 01:04:30.050
or a scriptorium or an illustrator.

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:33.040
This is unusual for many reasons

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:35.480
and I've done a study of
the images in this book,

01:04:35.480 --> 01:04:38.594
but this might be one of the only ones.

01:04:38.594 --> 01:04:41.580
There's a few in the 17th
century when they were engraved

01:04:41.580 --> 01:04:43.110
but as far as my script
says, you can see this,

01:04:43.110 --> 01:04:46.520
the birth of Adonis,
coming out of the tree.

01:04:46.520 --> 01:04:49.740
So there's all these classical
references to these things.

01:04:49.740 --> 01:04:52.693
They tend to be right at the
very beginnings of every piece,

01:04:53.750 --> 01:04:56.633
and then later on the animals
start attacking each other.

01:04:58.990 --> 01:05:00.933
You see a lot of these kinds of,

01:05:02.723 --> 01:05:07.723
this decoration that you
find in Venetian books

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:12.360
or* automated books, all this kinda stuff.

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:14.817
So there's a strong
influence from the Venetian,

01:05:14.817 --> 01:05:17.080
*Arabasks, I think is what they're called

01:05:17.980 --> 01:05:18.980
from that tradition.

01:05:20.211 --> 01:05:21.610
Good, good question.

01:05:21.610 --> 01:05:25.693
- This is gorgeous, wow, very gorgeous.

01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:30.470
I feel bad that we've been
monopolizing the entire time

01:05:30.470 --> 01:05:31.333
for questions.

01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:35.600
Let's wait and see if
there are any questions

01:05:35.600 --> 01:05:39.200
from the audience, please
go ahead and type them,

01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:40.223
in the Q and A.

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:49.030
I could keep asking trying to understand,

01:05:49.030 --> 01:05:51.243
this stuff is so, so fascinating.

01:05:52.460 --> 01:05:54.930
So I guess while we wait for someone else

01:05:54.930 --> 01:05:59.113
to muster up courage
to type up a question,

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:03.223
I would like to sort of continue.

01:06:06.270 --> 01:06:08.920
You showed a lot of
these manuscripts, right?

01:06:08.920 --> 01:06:13.920
So what, in modern day
music, what do you think,

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:18.106
how should we preserve our
musical traditions now?

01:06:18.106 --> 01:06:19.273
- Well, they're all digital.

01:06:21.640 --> 01:06:24.170
How do you preserve the actual item

01:06:24.170 --> 01:06:25.630
or preserve the traditions,

01:06:25.630 --> 01:06:28.610
say, a tradition that in a village

01:06:28.610 --> 01:06:31.570
that's rapidly being modernized
or something like that?

01:06:31.570 --> 01:06:33.720
- Both, I mean, both really, right.

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:36.660
You have a recording that is digital.

01:06:36.660 --> 01:06:41.660
But the text, the actual
musical scoring of it and so on,

01:06:43.730 --> 01:06:45.373
I mean, just scanned copies.

01:06:47.871 --> 01:06:51.050
Velom has survived quite well.

01:06:51.050 --> 01:06:52.930
- It's not much anymore.

01:06:52.930 --> 01:06:55.650
I think that the large
collections in Europe

01:06:55.650 --> 01:06:58.610
have been on a project of
digitizing now for 20 years

01:06:58.610 --> 01:06:59.940
and during the pandemic,

01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:03.610
it was remarkable to see
how I could continue my work

01:07:03.610 --> 01:07:07.520
by being able to get complete
full text manuscripts

01:07:07.520 --> 01:07:09.803
from most of the major libraries.

01:07:11.444 --> 01:07:13.010
There's enormous amount
of government funding,

01:07:13.010 --> 01:07:15.170
I've just signed off as a reviewer

01:07:15.170 --> 01:07:17.453
for a very large one in Germany,

01:07:18.300 --> 01:07:21.040
that's probably the largest
one that I've ever seen,

01:07:21.040 --> 01:07:23.120
with the Austrian National Library in tow

01:07:23.120 --> 01:07:25.250
and things like that, but that is a way,

01:07:25.250 --> 01:07:27.660
and those things are searchable.

01:07:27.660 --> 01:07:30.520
There's a lot of
metadata, there's tagging,

01:07:30.520 --> 01:07:35.393
it's easy to burn through
those collections,

01:07:36.852 --> 01:07:39.840
with questions and try to find stuff.

01:07:39.840 --> 01:07:42.600
So in that sense, it's very
good to have those digitized

01:07:42.600 --> 01:07:44.360
because for analytical work,

01:07:44.360 --> 01:07:46.743
it becomes much, much easier.

01:07:49.876 --> 01:07:50.709
But in the day,

01:07:50.709 --> 01:07:53.200
I mean, I looked at every
single one of these manuscripts

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:55.270
by hand, and most of
my colleagues did too,

01:07:55.270 --> 01:07:56.103
there was no other way.

01:07:56.103 --> 01:07:57.903
Or you looked at it on microfilm,

01:07:58.906 --> 01:08:00.460
those days are over.

01:08:00.460 --> 01:08:03.790
As far as the musical
traditions themselves,

01:08:03.790 --> 01:08:08.790
I mean the older, older
history of ethnic musicology

01:08:09.410 --> 01:08:12.320
was that you would try to
preserve these traditions,

01:08:12.320 --> 01:08:15.440
but by preserving them,
you're also recording them

01:08:15.440 --> 01:08:18.810
and you're creating a kind of intrusion.

01:08:18.810 --> 01:08:21.740
Alan Lomax recorded all
of those blues musicians

01:08:21.740 --> 01:08:23.660
in the south, country blues players,

01:08:23.660 --> 01:08:27.180
musicologists went to
Bali, they went to India,

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:28.463
recorded and recorded.

01:08:30.630 --> 01:08:33.020
And then as tourism encroaches,

01:08:33.020 --> 01:08:35.100
and you see some of these villages

01:08:35.100 --> 01:08:36.610
now stopping to play music

01:08:36.610 --> 01:08:38.520
and those musicians who
played village music

01:08:38.520 --> 01:08:40.020
are now playing in the hotels.

01:08:42.110 --> 01:08:44.150
You could decry that and say, wow,

01:08:44.150 --> 01:08:45.690
this is so disappointing.

01:08:45.690 --> 01:08:47.830
But what you're seeing is
a transformative aspect

01:08:47.830 --> 01:08:49.870
of how music is being transformed.

01:08:49.870 --> 01:08:51.433
Goa's another great example.

01:08:53.329 --> 01:08:56.827
The whole coastline is
dotted with five star hotels

01:08:56.827 --> 01:08:59.375
and you go in there and you find people

01:08:59.375 --> 01:09:04.375
singing in konkani,
traditional konkani songs,

01:09:05.040 --> 01:09:06.190
you know, for tourists.

01:09:07.520 --> 01:09:09.690
The interest now is how
those things have changed

01:09:09.690 --> 01:09:11.340
from early recordings.

01:09:11.340 --> 01:09:14.460
What we are finding out
is in places that play,

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:16.400
Golan instruments and things like that,

01:09:16.400 --> 01:09:18.850
all these hotel players
are playing much faster

01:09:18.850 --> 01:09:20.070
than they ever had before,

01:09:20.070 --> 01:09:21.120
because they've gotta fit it in

01:09:21.120 --> 01:09:23.160
for the people that arrive on the bus

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:24.540
where they're stopped at the hotel

01:09:24.540 --> 01:09:27.930
to have some food and hear the show

01:09:27.930 --> 01:09:30.480
and then go back on the
bus to the next event.

01:09:30.480 --> 01:09:31.860
So everything has been transformed.

01:09:31.860 --> 01:09:33.217
So in a sense, you have
the musicologists saying,

01:09:33.217 --> 01:09:34.163
let it die,

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:39.255
and see how it see how
it plays itself out.

01:09:39.255 --> 01:09:41.830
- Right. And see what
remains as a residue.

01:09:41.830 --> 01:09:45.390
- Right. Rather than the
old, * alamirium concept

01:09:45.390 --> 01:09:47.820
of the knight on the white horse,

01:09:47.820 --> 01:09:50.273
who's gonna come in and
record and save everyone.

01:09:52.000 --> 01:09:53.500
That's the white night theory.

01:09:55.870 --> 01:09:57.963
- Great, thank you so much.

01:09:58.900 --> 01:09:59.733
I think Ty,

01:09:59.733 --> 01:10:03.023
did you have any last
questions before we wrap up?

01:10:04.010 --> 01:10:06.320
- I was curious, I'm not sure if this

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:08.820
is historically correct

01:10:08.820 --> 01:10:13.820
but what, from what you
presented, flourished most

01:10:14.199 --> 01:10:16.253
in the new world when it was found.

01:10:17.460 --> 01:10:18.880
What kind of made it over?

01:10:18.880 --> 01:10:20.073
Were there any, well,

01:10:23.180 --> 01:10:26.410
- In India, they brought books of chants.

01:10:26.410 --> 01:10:28.160
The early explorers brought books of chant

01:10:28.160 --> 01:10:30.650
because they needed to
celebrate mass on the ship.

01:10:30.650 --> 01:10:32.730
And when they got there and
they built a very early church

01:10:32.730 --> 01:10:35.560
in St. Catherine's, which still exists

01:10:35.560 --> 01:10:37.763
crumbling in old Goa.

01:10:39.030 --> 01:10:43.707
By 1540, they were bringing in polyphony.

01:10:45.860 --> 01:10:48.090
So this is a chant now with another voice.

01:10:48.090 --> 01:10:49.830
I don't know what kind,
it's probably very simple,

01:10:49.830 --> 01:10:53.183
two voice polyphony, and
they have a word for it.

01:10:54.227 --> 01:10:58.470
*conto d'organo , singing over the organ,

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:02.070
so two voices versus
*conto chao which is chant.

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:04.190
So that's a big development there.

01:11:04.190 --> 01:11:08.120
By the 17th century
mainstream Jesuit music,

01:11:08.120 --> 01:11:09.993
because the Jesuits were in Goa.

01:11:11.030 --> 01:11:13.900
Mainstream, Roman Jesuit
music from the Roman college

01:11:13.900 --> 01:11:16.053
is being sung in Goa, mainstream.

01:11:17.810 --> 01:11:21.710
Going the other way, there's a very large,

01:11:21.710 --> 01:11:24.940
two very large collections in Mexico city

01:11:24.940 --> 01:11:29.940
that are late 16th, early 17th
century, mainstream music.

01:11:30.160 --> 01:11:32.920
But also containing some composers

01:11:32.920 --> 01:11:34.140
that were trained in Mexico.

01:11:34.140 --> 01:11:35.160
Some of the indigenous composers

01:11:35.160 --> 01:11:36.560
that were trained in Mexico.

01:11:39.060 --> 01:11:41.543
That's been a very
important source for study,

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:46.033
There is a lot of travel,

01:11:48.370 --> 01:11:51.220
this kind of music, all of it
required extensive training

01:11:52.510 --> 01:11:56.023
and training requires an
extensive commitment of resources.

01:11:57.040 --> 01:12:01.158
So in a sense in the end
you talk about India,

01:12:01.158 --> 01:12:04.490
the Christian project
really did not survive.

01:12:04.490 --> 01:12:06.950
- But not take off really...

01:12:06.950 --> 01:12:08.470
- It didn't really, all the letters

01:12:08.470 --> 01:12:11.580
that Xavier sent back to Rome

01:12:11.580 --> 01:12:14.070
saying that he had baptized 10,000 people

01:12:14.070 --> 01:12:15.870
on the coast and things like that,

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:18.200
he went to Kerala and
baptized another 5,000.

01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:19.703
Those were all exaggerations.

01:12:21.760 --> 01:12:24.090
- We do have one question in the Q and A

01:12:24.090 --> 01:12:25.140
if I may read it out.

01:12:26.240 --> 01:12:29.600
Was there any music
related to specific events,

01:12:29.600 --> 01:12:33.890
historical figures or memories
that people could interpret

01:12:33.890 --> 01:12:35.113
in their own ways?

01:12:37.780 --> 01:12:38.973
- Well, there's a lot of music

01:12:38.973 --> 01:12:40.990
that was written for specific events.

01:12:40.990 --> 01:12:42.683
These would be what we have,

01:12:43.765 --> 01:12:48.765
large events, institutional
events, coronation, weddings,

01:12:51.300 --> 01:12:53.400
et cetera, weddings, and things like that.

01:12:54.575 --> 01:12:59.030
We do see that music
begin to then trickle down

01:12:59.030 --> 01:13:03.200
into the hands of domestic musicians.

01:13:03.200 --> 01:13:06.640
So that's a good point and one example

01:13:06.640 --> 01:13:08.940
would be Medici festival music,

01:13:08.940 --> 01:13:13.430
these enormous pageant celebrations
with tons of pageantry,

01:13:13.430 --> 01:13:15.260
which went on for weeks
and weeks at a time

01:13:15.260 --> 01:13:17.010
when one of the Medici married somebody

01:13:17.010 --> 01:13:19.270
from the Habsburg empire or

01:13:19.270 --> 01:13:21.463
from the French empire or whatever.

01:13:23.620 --> 01:13:25.820
Eventually that music
began to trickle down,

01:13:26.700 --> 01:13:28.640
and I've traced a little
bit of how it was,

01:13:28.640 --> 01:13:30.310
but it got into the hands
of domestic players,

01:13:30.310 --> 01:13:32.040
people who were probably connected

01:13:32.040 --> 01:13:34.440
with seven degrees of
separation from the court.

01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:37.130
And then that music was arranged

01:13:37.130 --> 01:13:40.660
and some of that music which
was originally for four voices

01:13:40.660 --> 01:13:45.520
now appears for one
voice and *ludacontinum .

01:13:45.520 --> 01:13:47.390
So that's what actually happens.

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:51.359
So that is a transformation,
a personal transformation

01:13:51.359 --> 01:13:55.253
of a work for that person's own use.

01:13:58.740 --> 01:14:02.350
- Great, we should probably
not detain you any longer

01:14:02.350 --> 01:14:06.560
and thanks once again Victor
for the wonderful talk.

01:14:09.145 --> 01:14:10.544
- I'll see you in May, yes.

01:14:10.544 --> 01:14:13.990
- Yeah. I'll see you in May
and I'm really looking forward

01:14:13.990 --> 01:14:16.180
to when we can welcome you to campus.

01:14:16.180 --> 01:14:17.013
- Thank you, I appreciate that.

01:14:17.013 --> 01:14:19.770
Thank you very much and thank
you everyone for listening.

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:20.900
- Thank you Victor, thank you.

01:14:20.900 --> 01:14:24.530
- I also want to say thanks to Dr. Ty Kamp

01:14:24.530 --> 01:14:28.220
and Brian McEvoy for helping
us with the recording

